1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:18,000
Artifacts are signals or distortions not generated by cardiac
electrical activity. They can mimic dangerous rhythms or obscure real
ones. Begin with the patient, then compare the tracing with pulse and
perfusion, other leads and any visible source of movement.

2
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:36,552
An alert patient with an effective pulse does not have sustained VF
merely because one lead is chaotic. Conversely, artifact can coexist
with genuine arrest. If circulation is absent, initiate the
appropriate response while checking equipment without prolonged
interruptions.

3
00:00:36,552 --> 00:00:56,808
Motion artifact often produces large, erratic deflections around
patient movement, procedures or transport. Muscle tremor can produce
fine rapid oscillation, for example with shivering or Parkinsonian
movement. Either can imitate rhythm abnormalities, so appearance alone
is not enough.

4
00:00:56,808 --> 00:01:16,920
Look for underlying QRS complexes that maintain their timing and
compare another lead with the patient’s pulse. Support painful limbs,
warm a cold patient and offer reassurance. Improvement after these
actions supports an artifact explanation but does not erase a
coexisting clinical problem.

5
00:01:16,920 --> 00:01:35,880
Baseline wander is slow movement of the reference line, commonly
associated with breathing or poor contact. A loose electrode can
create intermittent signal loss or a flat segment. Neither pattern
proves a true atrial rhythm or asystole until patient and equipment
have been assessed.

6
00:01:35,880 --> 00:01:55,584
Check the electrode site, adhesive, cable and connection. Dry sweat
and remove interfering hair using appropriate equipment. Use the
manufacturer-approved skin preparation and allow the site to dry
before applying a fresh electrode. Record the correction and whether
the repeat tracing improved.

7
00:01:55,584 --> 00:02:16,680
In this setting, mains-related sixty-hertz interference may create a
dense fuzzy baseline. Inspect nearby power cords, chargers and
equipment, and keep signal cables away from likely interference
sources. Do not assume every fuzzy baseline is VF or every flat-
looking segment is true asystole.

8
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:36,312
Use safe equipment arrangements and device instructions; do not
disconnect essential monitoring or treatment simply to improve a
tracing. If a change in equipment position or power arrangement is
appropriate, reassess signal quality and document the result.
Persistent uncertainty belongs in the handover.

9
00:02:36,312 --> 00:02:54,672
The school contrasts tremor with VF, baseline wander with flutter and
movement with tachyarrhythmia. These are comparison exercises, not
absolute rules. True tachyarrhythmias can be irregular; artifact can
affect several leads; illness and movement can occur together.

10
00:02:54,672 --> 00:03:12,864
Combine clinical pulse status, repeated acquisition, lead consistency
and whether underlying complexes remain visible. A poor signal limits
confidence. If you cannot distinguish the rhythm reliably, state that
clearly and obtain appropriate assistance while continuing care.

11
00:03:12,864 --> 00:03:34,320
Preparation includes dignity, anatomical landmarks and secure contact,
not just pressing the acquisition button. Position the patient as
clinically appropriate and obtain a stationary recording when
feasible. For suspected ACS, efficient preparation helps achieve the
school’s timely diagnostic acquisition target.

12
00:03:34,320 --> 00:03:52,128
If the first tracing remains unreliable, repeat after a targeted
correction rather than changing many variables without recording what
happened. Preserve clinically important earlier tracings and their
times so the receiving team can distinguish technical change from
genuine evolution.

13
00:03:52,128 --> 00:04:10,008
Document the artifact, why it prevented reliable interpretation, the
corrective action and the outcome of the repeat. Include symptoms and
perfusion at the same time. Saying only that the second ECG was normal
hides the reason the earlier tracing looked different.

14
00:04:10,008 --> 00:04:28,248
A complete handover might say that movement obscured atrial activity,
contact and positioning were corrected, and a repeat showed a
describable rhythm while symptoms persisted. The repeat improves
electrical evidence but does not, by itself, resolve the patient’s
complaint.
